  ☑ Add configurable resolution for atmospheric LUT.
    - Atmosphere.SkyLutQuality and ConfigLib selector retain the 32x24 default; each level increases
      both dimensions by their base size (1x through 4x). Full LUT rebuilds now run asynchronously, retaining the previous
      displayed LUT until coherent texture/lighting replacement on the render thread. SIMD batches
      remain bounded to 128 directions. The original quality validation passed 47 focused tests. Validation
      evidence and configuration details: PBR.Atmosphere.md.
  ☐ Make atmospheric shader look better and more physically accurate.
    - Preserve full-LUT background computation, bounded SIMD scratch storage, cancellation on teardown,
      and coherent render-thread publication while displaying the previous complete result.
    - Reference: docs/PBR.Atmosphere.md and Hillaire's atmosphere technique:
      https://sebh.github.io/publications/egsr2020.pdf. Record approximation choices and measured costs.
    ✔ Add multiple scattering to atmospheric radiance and shared sky lighting. @done(26-09-27 03:01)
      - Implement a bounded LUT-based multiple-scattering approximation, including the chosen ground
        reflectance contribution; retain consistent units and avoid counting single scattering twice.
      - Feed the resulting radiance into both the visible sky and shared environment lighting.
        Validate clear/hazy skies, twilight, altitude changes and energy behavior against a reference.
      - Implemented: cached isotropic source/feedback LUT (32x16 through 128x64 under shared
        atmospheric quality), quality-scaled angular/ray/light integration budgets, analytic
        segment integration and uniform Lambertian ground albedo 0.1. The same added radiance feeds
        the sky and shared environment/horizon lighting; direct solar irradiance remains unchanged.
        Numerical validation and independent review are recorded in PBR.Atmosphere.md.
        User-run appearance acceptance remains open; no live visual result is claimed.
    ☑ SIMD-accelerate multiple-scattering table construction with TensorPrimitives.
      - Batch independent angular rays and their solar transmittance integration through the existing
        width-independent TensorPrimitives approach; preserve isotropic normalization, analytic segment
        integration, ground reflection and the geometric-series energy bound.
      - Use bounded pooled scratch storage and cancellation between batches. Retain a scalar reference
        for numerical checks; avoid register-width-specific implementations and per-ray allocations.
      - Measure matched table dimensions and integration budgets, separating cold table construction
        from cached sky refresh. Cover every quality budget with bounded reference workloads and report
        accuracy, allocations and worker latency before adopting the optimized implementation.
      - Implemented: 128-ray tensor batches with pooled scratch, compacted solar paths, invariant
        direction reuse and scalar reference comparisons. Passed 94 focused atmosphere tests and
        nine hardware-intrinsics-disabled cases; independent review found no defects. Matched default
        table builds measured about 7x faster with identical returned-table allocation. Full evidence
        and bounded higher-quality measurements: PBR.Atmosphere.md.
    ☑ Port atmospheric sky-model computation to GPU compute shaders with CPU fallback.
      - Compute the multiple-scattering source table, sky radiance LUT and matching shared lighting
        integrals on supported GPUs. Preserve the current physical model, quality budgets, weather
        quantization and cache invalidation; retain the CPU implementation as the numerical reference
        and asynchronous fallback for GPUs lacking the required capabilities or resource limits.
      - Use the existing GL capabilities, GPU compute/resource abstractions, shader contracts and
        production SPIR-V build pipeline. Keep shader sources in shader assets and select the backend
        explicitly without per-sample capability branches.
      - Bound dispatch work and temporary storage, order dependent passes with the required barriers,
        and publish only after GPU completion. Keep the previous complete sky and lighting active;
        use existing asynchronous readback abstractions for CPU consumers without render-thread waits.
      - Keep LUT resources and shared lighting in one coherent generation across quality/weather
        changes, shader reloads and world teardown. Retire in-flight resources through existing GPU
        lifetime abstractions and prevent obsolete completions from publishing into a new world.
      - Validate GPU/CPU numerical agreement across quality levels, clear/hazy skies, twilight,
        altitude extremes and ground reflection. Exercise unsupported-GPU fallback and lifecycle
          transitions; measure GPU time, CPU overhead, memory and publication latency at matched budgets.
        - Implemented asset-backed SPIR-V source/sky/lighting passes, bounded fenced dispatches, coherent
          readback/publication and automatic CPU fallback. 124 distinct focused checks passed; all 30
          GPU/queue checks also passed in Release. Matched default Release GPU execution: 0.721–0.778 ms
          for a source rebuild, 0.334–0.361 ms with cached source. Full quality-3 GPU execution and peak
          dispatch were measured; full quality-3 CPU timing and live terrain contention remain unmeasured.
          Ownership, storage bounds, receipts and frame-cadence limits: PBR.Atmosphere.GpuComputation.md.
    ☑ Derive atmospheric parameters from world calendar, player latitude and seasonal world conditions.
      - Trace the base-game calendar, axial tilt, world-relative latitude and climate APIs, including
        coordinate conventions and hemisphere behavior. Reuse its solar direction/season model without
        applying the planet's tilt a second time.
        - Replace fixed groundAlbedo with an approximation of regional snow coverage from average
          temperature and seasonal warming/cooling direction. Do not scan actual terrain, blocks or
          snow-cover data. Model accumulation delay and persistence during spring melt; use a documented
          neutral fallback when regional climate is unavailable. Reuse the latitude-aware calendar/climate
          model without applying axial tilt again or tinting reflectance directly by solar angle.
      - Identify other physically relevant seasonal inputs supported by world data (such as aerosol or
        atmospheric density); document their mappings and avoid inventing changes to fixed physical constants.
      - Capture an immutable input snapshot on the game thread for background computation. Extend cache
        identity and stable quantization to all transport-affecting parameters, preserving coherent
        publication and avoiding rebuild churn from tiny time/position changes.
        - Validate opposing hemispheres, solstices/equinoxes, latitude transitions, snow-cover changes,
          time jumps and world teardown; record CPU costs and obtain user-run seasonal appearance checks.
        - Implementation and automated validation complete: climate/calendar-only thermal-lag snow
          estimate, immutable capture, shared 0.02 reflectance buckets in CPU/GPU transport and cache
          keys. Release atmosphere suite: 114 passed, five optional measurements skipped. Strict mocks
          forbid terrain scans. Capture costs are bounded to one climate lookup plus 36 date evaluations
          (12 with a pinned season), with zero climate calls on cache hits. Mock timings are not engine
            cost evidence; real callback cost and seasonal appearance remain unverified. Marked complete
            at user direction; those observations are not completion blockers.
          Source audit, approximation limits and validation: PBR.Atmosphere.Seasons.md.
